  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper ridge remains centered over Texas today, while a tight\neasterly wave sits within this ridge, gradually drifting west\nover far west Texas and northern Mexico this afternoon. Mean\nflow aloft is mainly southwesterly today, which has contributed\nto a relative reduction in moisture across southeastern Arizona.\nEarly afternoon surface dew points range from the upper 40s to\nmid 50s with precipitable water values at about normal for this\ntime of year. While the wave to the east remains well away from\nthe area today and with no other real triggering mechanisms,\nmonsoon activity will remain limited today to mainly high\nterrain areas, especially closer to the New Mexico border. Given\nthe lack of coverage, the 12Z HREF shows little in the way of\na signal for monsoon hazards, but a quick 40-45 mph wind gust\nand heavy downpour can't be ruled out with any storm today. \n\nUpper level support for increased thunderstorm activity becomes\na more favorable on Monday as the easterly wave approaches and\nan upper high drifts out of Sonora west over Baja. No real\nchange in the moisture profile is expected, but this is enough\nto produce 15-30 percent chances Monday afternoon and evening\nmainly from eastern Pima/Pinal eastward through the New Mexico\nborder and south through Santa Cruz/Cochise. Mean wind will have\nturned to come out of the northeast, with thunderstorms forming\nover the White Mountains and other high terrain areas likely to\nmove south/southwest with a gusty wind and potentially a blowing\ndust threat. The 12Z HREF highlights the potential for 35-45 kt\nwinds off the White Mountains into Greenlee-Graham-Pinal\ncounties with more isolated chances further south.\n\nThe upper ridge originally centered to the east will gradually\nbuild into the Great Basin through the week, with positive\nheight anomalies increasing over Arizona. This will bring\nunseasonably warm temperatures with highs 5-7 degrees above\nnormal. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect Monday through\nFriday for portions of southeastern Arizona. This northward push\nof the ridge will also allow for a more favorable monsoon\npattern by the second half of the week.",
